(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-bacterial-infections-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cutaneous Bacterial Infections Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Describe the classic appearance of non-bullous impetigo.","choices":["A macule evolves into a vesicle or pustule that ruptures, leaving a honey-coloured adherent crust","Tense clear or cloudy bullae appear on intact skin and leave a collarette after they rupture","A vesicle over inflamed skin deepens into a dermal ulcer with a thick grey-yellow crust","Papules and pustules on an erythematous base, each pierced by a single central hair"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, typically on the face and extremities, with regional lymphadenopathy common. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What distinguishes ecthyma from ordinary impetigo?","choices":["It spreads rapidly along superficial lymphatics with a sharply demarcated raised edge","It deepens into a dermal ulceration with a thicker grey-yellow crust, heals slowly and scars","It remains confined to the epidermis, resolves within a week and never leaves a scar behind","It produces tense bullae on intact skin that rupture to leave shallow moist erosions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, usually on the lower extremities, with regional lymphadenopathy common. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"Which oral agents are named for folliculitis when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is cultured or suspected?","choices":["Cephalexin, dicloxacillin or amoxicillin-clavulanate","Penicillin V, clindamycin or erythromycin base","Doxycycline, minocycline or sarecycline","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, ciprofloxacin or linezolid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin and dicloxacillin cover the methicillin-sensitive organism. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotic is specifically suggested for paronychia after nail biting?","choices":["Clindamycin, because of exposure to oral flora","Doxycycline, because of exposure to oral flora","Ciprofloxacin, because of exposure to oral flora","Penicillin V, because of exposure to oral flora"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Amoxicillin-clavulanate and cephalexin are the other named options. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 119"},{"q":"What is the treatment of necrotizing fasciitis?","choices":["Warm compresses with elevation of the limb and a narrow-spectrum oral antibiotic course","Aggressive surgical debridement with broad antibiotic cover and surgical intensive care admission","Broad intravenous antibiotics alone with observation on a general medical ward for forty-eight hours","Incision and drainage under local anaesthetic followed by discharge on oral antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — cover aerobic Gram-positive and Gram-negative organisms and anaerobes, with a team approach. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 131"},{"q":"Why should topical tretinoin and benzoyl peroxide not be applied at the same time?","choices":["Because tretinoin blocks the antimicrobial effect that benzoyl peroxide has","Because of the risk of skin irritation, so at least three hours should separate them","Because benzoyl peroxide inactivates tretinoin, so alternate days are recommended","Because the combination raises the risk of photosensitivity in treated areas"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That interval is specified in the patient education. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"How is erythrasma treated?","choices":["Dilute acetic acid compresses if localised, oral ciprofloxacin if the disease is widespread","Topical antifungal alone if localised, oral fluconazole if the disease proves severe","Topical erythromycin or clindamycin if localised, oral erythromycin or clarithromycin if widespread","Topical mupirocin if localised, oral dicloxacillin or cephalexin if the disease is widespread"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An antifungal cream such as miconazole is added if yeast is also present. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"By what mechanisms do oral contraceptive pills improve acne?","choices":["Progestin blocks the androgen receptor within the sebaceous gland and reduces keratinisation","Estrogen suppresses Cutibacterium acnes directly and reduces follicular hyperkeratinisation","Estrogen decreases sebum, suppresses ovarian androgens and raises sex hormone binding globulin","Estrogen increases sebum, stimulates ovarian androgens and lowers sex hormone binding globulin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Raising that globulin lowers circulating free testosterone. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What are the examination findings of cellulitis?","choices":["Tense bullae with purple fluid and skin that has become insensate over the affected area","All four cardinal signs of inflammation, most often on one lower leg, with borders neither raised nor demarcated","All four cardinal signs of inflammation, most often bilateral, with a sharply raised and demarcated edge","Painless erythema with scaling at the border, most often over intertriginous skin surfaces"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral disease is described as almost never occurring. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotics are used for furuncles and carbuncles when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is cultured or suspected?","choices":["Ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin or moxifloxacin","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin or doxycycline","Dicloxacillin, cephalexin or amoxicillin-clavulanate","Penicillin V, erythromycin or clarithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Empiric therapy for the sensitive organism is dicloxacillin or cephalexin. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which patients are given as examples of high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus in the impetigo section?","choices":["A health-care worker or a teacher","A child under three or an infant","A swimmer or a laundry worker","A bartender or a professional cook"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Culture is obtained in those patients rather than in every case. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which intervention offers the best chance of permanent cure in hidradenitis suppurativa?","choices":["Long-term systemic antibiotic therapy","Intralesional triamcinolone injections","Infliximab given for severe disease","Wide excision of the affected areas"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Large fluctuant cysts are meanwhile incised and drained. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Why is spironolactone used in hidradenitis suppurativa?","choices":["It is an immunosuppressant that blocks tumour necrosis factor in inflamed tissue","It is a retinoid that reduces sebum production and follicular hyperkeratinisation","It is a corticosteroid that shrinks draining sinuses when injected into a lesion","It is an aldosterone antagonist that inhibits ovarian and adrenal androgen production"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Combination birth control pills act by reducing gonadotropin release. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"How does the sex distribution of acne differ between adolescence and adulthood?","choices":["Adolescent acne is more common and severe in males; post-adolescent acne is more common in women","Adolescent acne is more common and severe in females; post-adolescent acne is more common in men","Adolescent acne affects both sexes equally; post-adolescent acne is far more common in men","Adolescent acne is more common in males; post-adolescent acne affects both sexes about equally"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Post-adolescent acne is defined here as acne over 25 years of age. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is the hallmark lesion of acne vulgaris on physical examination?","choices":["The pustule, sitting on an erythematous base around a central hair","The nodule, deep-seated and arising in several adjacent follicles","The papule, small and inflamed without any surface keratin plug","The comedone, either open (blackhead) or closed (whitehead)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acne is polymorphic, but the comedone is what defines it. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which preventive measures are advised in hidradenitis suppurativa?","choices":["Avoid all water contact, apply a barrier ointment daily, gain weight and use an occlusive dressing","Shower immediately after any hot tub use and monitor the disinfectant levels in the water","Avoid sharing towels or razors and isolate from school until treatment has been underway","Avoid heat, cleanse daily with antibacterial soap or benzoyl peroxide wash, lose weight, stop smoking"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, along with avoiding constrictive clothing and considering laser hair removal. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Describe the physical findings of a carbuncle.","choices":["Nodules, sinus tracts and meshlike scarring in the axilla or the perineum","A well-demarcated raised plaque with red streaks extending toward a lymph node","Several loculated abscesses, superficial pustules, necrotic plugs and sieve-like draining openings","A single firm tender nodule with one opening and a surrounding zone of erythema and swelling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a deeper infection made of interconnecting furuncles in several follicles. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which layer does erysipelas involve, and which organism causes it?","choices":["The upper dermis and superficial cutaneous lymphatics, most often Group A streptococcus","The deeper dermis and subcutaneous tissue, most often Staphylococcus aureus","The superficial epidermis only, most often Streptococcus pyogenes or staphylococci","The fascia surrounding muscle and nerve, most often a polymicrobial mixture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is a superficial cellulitis of the dermal layer. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"How does a cutaneous abscess differ in origin from a furuncle?","choices":["An abscess arises from an obstructed apocrine gland, whereas a furuncle follows a break in the epidermis","An abscess arises from lymphatic spread of streptococci, whereas a furuncle spreads along the dermis","An abscess usually follows traumatic inoculation of bacteria, whereas a furuncle arises from an infected follicle","An abscess arises from an infected hair follicle, whereas a furuncle follows traumatic inoculation of bacteria"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Abscesses are often polymicrobial, with Staphylococcus aureus the commonest organism. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"Which organism causes erythrasma and where does it live?","choices":["Staphylococcus aureus, releasing epidermolytic toxins that split the epidermis","Corynebacterium minutissimum, invading the upper third of the stratum corneum","Candida albicans, colonising the space beneath a separated proximal nail fold","Pseudomonas aeruginosa, entering the follicle from inadequately chlorinated water"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Heat and humidity are the favourable conditions; diabetics are high risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What is the pharmacologic treatment of erysipelas?","choices":["Penicillin V, with clindamycin if the patient is penicillin allergic","Cephalexin, with trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole if purulence is present","Mupirocin ointment, with oral dicloxacillin if lesions are widespread","Doxycycline, with benzoyl peroxide added to reduce bacterial resistance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prompt treatment matters because progression can be rapid. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which lesion type is characteristic of hidradenitis suppurativa on examination?","choices":["The tense clear bulla that leaves a collarette after rupture","The double comedone — a blackhead with two or more openings","The honey-coloured adherent crust overlying a superficial erosion","The follicular pustule pierced by a single central hair shaft"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside nodules, sinus tracts, abscesses and meshlike scarring. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What is hidradenitis suppurativa and where does it occur?","choices":["Infection of the hair follicle extending into the adjacent subcutaneous fat of the neck and back","Inflammation of cutaneous apocrine glands, most often in the axilla, and also groin, breasts or perineum","Inflammation of the eccrine sweat glands, most often on the palms, soles and the axillary vault","Infection of the upper third of the stratum corneum in intertriginous skin under heat and humidity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is also called acne inversa because it resembles acne vulgaris. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which finding in a woman with persistent acne points toward polycystic ovarian syndrome?","choices":["Flushing with hot drinks and visible facial telangiectasias","Pustules pierced by a central hair over the thighs and trunk","Premenstrual flares of acne with an otherwise normal cycle","Hirsutism together with irregular or absent menses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That combination indicates androgen hypersecretion. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Where do furuncles and carbuncles typically occur?","choices":["In areas prone to friction or minor trauma — back of neck, face, axillae and buttocks","In intertriginous areas kept warm and moist — inner thighs, scrotum and toe webs","In areas bearing apocrine glands — axilla, groin, breasts and perineum","On the lower extremity in the great majority of cases, rarely bilateral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Shaving and insect bites are named traumatic risk factors. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which three factors predispose to recurrent furunculosis?","choices":["Obesity, diabetes and nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus","Smoking, hot weather and obstruction of the apocrine ducts","Impaired lymphatic drainage, tinea pedis and previous impetigo","Manicure, ingrown nail and the habit of biting the nails"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Immunocompromise and trauma are the broader risk categories. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is the pathophysiology of pseudofolliculitis barbae?","choices":["Keratin plugs occlude the pilosebaceous unit and sebum accumulates behind the blockage","Apocrine ducts become obstructed and rupture, seeding inflammation into the dermis","A cut hair curves into the follicular wall and penetrates the skin, causing a foreign body reaction","Bacteria enter the follicle through a break in the skin and produce a follicular pustule"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It occurs in any shaved area and commonly in Black males with tightly curled facial hair. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Why does devitalised tissue in cellulitis require surgical debridement?","choices":["It prevents accurate culture of the organism from the affected area","It causes pain that cannot be controlled with systemic analgesia","It is not perfused, so antibiotics cannot reach the site","It harbours resistant organisms that no oral antibiotic will cover"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — tense, cyanotic, necrotic, bronzed or blanched skin is the clue. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"Which statement about preventing hot tub folliculitis is correct?","choices":["Showering immediately after contact reliably prevents it if done within thirty minutes","Raising the water temperature above forty degrees kills the organism responsible","Wearing a rash guard in the water prevents the organism reaching the follicles","Showering after contact does not prevent it; continuous filtration and disinfectant monitoring do"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, along with frequent changing of the water. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which trait is shared by the oral agents named across this lecture for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus skin infection?","choices":["Penicillin V, erythromycin and clarithromycin recur as the core options","Mupirocin, retapamulin and fusidic acid recur as the core options","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, clindamycin and doxycycline recur as the core options","Dicloxacillin, cephalexin and amoxicillin-clavulanate recur as the core options"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, with linezolid and ciprofloxacin added in particular settings. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"}]};
